@*
    A PAGE'S ACTION ROW HAS ONE LOCATION, AND THIS IS THE ONE HOME FOR IT (2026-09-19).

    Reported from production against two hubs: "inconsistent location of the buttons. some are on
    top right… standardise the location of the action buttons." On /admin/feedback the Export and
    Refresh buttons sat in a band of their own BELOW the tab strip, left-aligned; on /admin/branches
    so did Add Branch. On every ordinary page the same kind of button sits on the title row, top
    right. Two locations for one thing, and the hubs had the odd one.

    THE CAUSE WAS STRUCTURAL, NOT COSMETIC. A hub owns the page header ("A hub owns the route; its
    sections own nothing" in CLAUDE.md), so a folded section hid its own title with
    `@@if (!Embedded)` — and was left rendering a `.page-header` that contained nothing but its
    buttons. `.page-header` is `justify-content: space-between`, so a lone child sits at the START,
    which is why they were on the left as well as in the wrong band. Right-aligning them with CSS
    would have fixed the alignment and kept the extra band, which is the actual complaint: a
    second header strip that spends a row of the page to hold two buttons.

    So the buttons MOVE, into the hub's own title row, using Blazor's section support: the hub
    renders `<SectionOutlet SectionId="QPageActions.Id" />` in its `.header-right`, and an embedded
    section renders its buttons into `<SectionContent>` with the same id. The framework resolves
    that across the render tree, which is why this is a SectionOutlet and not a cascading
    RenderFragment a section assigns during its own render — that shape re-renders the hub from
    inside the child's render pass, and a `RenderFragment` lambda is a new object every time, so
    the equality check that would stop the loop can never hold.

    ONE OUTLET, ONE FILLER. `Id` is a single static object, so exactly one section may be on screen
    at a time — which is what a tab strip guarantees. A page that needs two action groups puts them
    both in this component's `Actions`.

    Not embedded, it renders the same `.page-header` markup the page had before, so a section that
    ever gets its own route again is unaffected and every non-hub page keeps its band untouched.
*@

@if (Embedded)
{
    <SectionContent SectionId="QPageActions.Id">@Actions</SectionContent>
}
else
{
    <div class="page-header">
        @if (Title is not null)
        {
            <div class="header-content">@Title</div>
        }
        <div class="header-actions">@Actions</div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The one section id shared by every hub's outlet and every embedded section's content.
    /// A plain object rather than a string: a string id is a name two unrelated components can
    /// collide on by accident, and there is only ever one page-action slot on screen.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly object Id = new();

    /// <summary>True when a hub is hosting this section, so the buttons belong in the hub's band.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Embedded { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The title block — an h1 and its subtitle. Rendered only when this page owns its band.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The buttons. These follow the page: into the hub's band when embedded, otherwise this one.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Actions { get; set; }
}
